00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.789
Then we will have a Q&amp;A session.

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:49.850
For those of you who don't know about SRA,
we are the Sequence Read Archive from NCBI.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:56.679
We started in 2008 and pur primary mission
is to store and distribute next generation

00:01:56.679 --> 00:01:58.070
sequencing data.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:04.249
We accept all kinds of samples and all kinds
of data from different platforms.

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:11.000
The data is used in a variety of different
ways, from bioinformatics development to detection

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.710
of novel genes or microbial or viral species
even.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:22.330
Why did we move to the cloud and why did we
do it 12 years after it started?

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.690
SRA has been growing exponentially year after
year.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.390
We are over 14 petabytes of data at this point.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:38.080
We have over 25 PB of user submitted files
that we can share with the community.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.680
This is a lot of data.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.970
Moving it from Bethesda, Maryland to anywhere
in the world is technically complex task and

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.540
also time-consuming.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:55.490
Some of our users need to wait weeks or months
to just download data before they can do any

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.240
computational analysis.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.020
Sometimes they discover some data they downloaded
is not useful and they need to download more.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.900
This is a big barrier to doing quick analysis
on the data available.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:17.150
Because there are great benefits, we want
to speed this up for the scientist community.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:25.560
We went ahead and moved all 14 PB of SRA archival
data into the cloud.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.370
From there you can get faster access to this
data.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:35.430
You have powerful computer resources available
to you on demand.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.700
You can easily expand the storage you are
using in the cloud so you are never waiting

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.590
to get more storage.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.930
The cloud can be a great collaboration platform.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.220
You can have your collaborators who may be
in different parts of the world log into the

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:56.510
same system and run the same pipelines and
look at the same results whenever they want.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:04.020
You don't need to share those results individually
since everybody can login into the same server.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:10.350
What we have done so far is we moved all of
the SRA archival format to the cloud.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:12.300
That is available now.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:19.570
We also started moving the user submitted
files which is roughly 25 PB more of data.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.180
This is a new feature from SRA.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.440
Instead of using archival format, you can
get to the files that we received from the

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.440
submitters.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.500
We are always trying to be more efficient
and we want to be more efficient in the cloud.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.050
We started producing contigs ourselves.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:43.070
For now we only produce contigs for metegenomic
datasets, but we will be branching out to

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:48.530
RNA-seq genomic data sets and other types
as time goes on.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:55.420
In order to access this data we updated our
API which is the SRA toolkit to work in the

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.500
cloud.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.320
Instead of downloading data from Bethesda,
you will be accessing the data in our cloud

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.690
bucket.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.030
This will speed up file access.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:15.639
Another feature is we made available our metadata
in native cloud architecture.

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:22.680
For now this is just Google's BigQuery product
but this was a popular feature and we will

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.670
be moving all of this also to Amazons Athena,
now that we have received positive feedback

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:32.280
from the scientific community for this.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.530
The case studies I wanted to highlight was
the NCBI virus discovery codathon.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:43.750
Is a good example of cloud computing and the
benefits of cloud computing.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:51.740
And our interaction with the cloud customer
who wanted to do things in the cloud natively.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:58.410
The virus discovery codathon was started with
assumptions that there is a lot of viral data

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:02.580
available in meta-genomic data sets.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:08.080
We brought several teams together and the
goal was to build some analysis and processing

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.900
pipelines for the data sets.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:17.669
I won't be going into the details of the actual
code or what the results are but if you are

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:21.790
interested to read about it there is a publication
link you can follow.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:22.790
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31527408

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:28.230
What I want to talk about is the pre-computation
that had to happen.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:35.680
The assumption was that everybody would be
working on contigs and not raw data.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:45.760
What SRA did is we took 140,000 runs of metagenomic
data sets that were 7 GB or smaller and we

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.000
built contigs from that.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.980
This was either de novo assembly or guided
assembly.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:07:00.340
We ended up building 3.9 billion contigs out
of the data.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.850
In order to accomplish this we had to take
a two-step approach in computation.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:14.830
The first one was using 500 nodes or servers
that were only 2 cores and 8GB in memory.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:20.949
These are very small machines and some of
our cell phones are more powerful then this.

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:26.600
Of course we had to use larger machines for
the bigger files.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:33.370
We used 100 machines and they had 96 cores
and 300 GB of RAM so they are big and powerful

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.870
machines.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:37.340
Why did we do in the cloud?

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.200
It only took a couple weeks to do this in
the cloud.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.590
We knew that if we try to do this internally
at NCBI on our high computing cluster it would

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.860
take us several months to do this.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:55.890
You can see the bar change from a couple weeks
to several months of computing these contigs.

00:07:55.890 --> 00:08:05.020
That is because SRA is not the only resource
at NCBI, we share the cluster with other resources.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:10.210
Of course we would be queued up for computational
needs and this is something that some of you

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:16.260
might have faced before as well at your universities.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.860
In the cloud all of the compute is what we
want and at our convenience.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:28.360
What we learned from this experience is if
you want to get started faster in the cloud,

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.360
collaborations help.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:35.500
So if you are collaborating with bioinformaticians
or programmers who are familiar with cloud

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:38.399
architecture that can help you get started
faster.

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:41.099
You can do this on your own.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:45.600
But if you will do this on your own make sure
you start working in the small-scale first.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.009
This will help you learn the different technologies.

00:08:49.009 --> 00:08:53.699
If you are using known data sets that are
small and quick to compute on, you know what

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:55.209
the results should be.

00:08:55.209 --> 00:08:59.550
As you get more comfortable with technology,
expand slowly.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:06.110
Premature scaling to 100 or several hundred
machines, can lead to a large problem and

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.639
you won't know where to debug the problem.

00:09:09.639 --> 00:09:14.490
It's important to scale slowly so you can
understand what's happening and if you run

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:20.050
into issues you can understand where to look
for the problems.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:28.050
If you want to know what technologies we used
in our computation approach, that was Kubernetes,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:35.559
Docker images, and there was scripting using
Python/BASH and Snakemake.

00:09:35.559 --> 00:09:36.790
These are not the only technologies available.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.060
Cloud technologies have competitors.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:47.040
When you look into cloud technologies, some
other technologies may be a better fit for

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.980
you than the ones we used.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:57.339
We tried to make our compute cheaper so we
try to use preemptable or spot instances depending

00:09:57.339 --> 00:09:59.600
on which cloud vendor we used.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:05.589
Those are cheaper options that you should
look into.

00:10:05.589 --> 00:10:10.760
On the note section of this presentation I
added links to different resources that you

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.329
should check out.

00:10:13.329 --> 00:10:19.399
After the webinar feel free to look at those
to get more information about any of these

00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:22.319
different topics.

00:10:22.319 --> 00:10:27.290
The community use case I wanted to highlight
was from researcher or who was interested

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:32.870
in the evolutionary relationship of microbial
communities and marine environments.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:36.240
They were looking at marine metagenomic data
sets.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.990
What they really wanted to do was build up
an automatic pipeline to look through SRA

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.800
data and the pipeline would run in the cloud.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.940
They needed to be able to search for these
datasets quickly and efficiently.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:57.949
They wanted to be able to fine-tune their
search parameters for these data sets.

00:10:57.949 --> 00:11:04.180
The other need was to be able to know what
the microbial communities are within the data

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:05.529
sets.

00:11:05.529 --> 00:11:13.120
For this we thought that BigQuery or Amazon
Athena products would be a good fit so we

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.869
started doing testing in the BigQuery.

00:11:15.869 --> 00:11:19.809
We started out putting SRA metadata there.

00:11:19.809 --> 00:11:27.770
We put sample information where the sample
was collected from and what depth and all

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.449
of this metadata that users provide.

00:11:30.449 --> 00:11:35.119
We also had sequencing information of how
the sample was sequenced in terms of what

00:11:35.119 --> 00:11:37.450
platforms were used.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:44.819
Also statistic information such as the number
of bytes or the number of reads or bases available

00:11:44.819 --> 00:11:46.829
in that data set.

00:11:46.829 --> 00:11:55.150
This allowed the user to select high-quality
data sets from a particular platform or if

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:59.980
they wanted the run to be a certain size.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:06.699
For the second need which was to know the
taxonomic content of the run, for that we

00:12:06.699 --> 00:12:13.720
had to make the results from the SRA taxonomic
analysis tool available in BigQuery.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:19.639
We haven't released this publicly yet as we
were still going through testing with a small

00:12:19.639 --> 00:12:25.889
user group, but we will be releasing this
soon and this functionality will be copied

00:12:25.889 --> 00:12:32.570
to Amazons Athena as well given more time.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:38.050
With this data set they can identify what
microbial communities are available in a particular

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.050
run.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:43.569
This is so they can fine tune their search
parameters even more.

00:12:43.569 --> 00:12:50.989
Since this information is available quickly
from BigQuery, they can take the sequencing

00:12:50.989 --> 00:12:56.949
information, they can take the search results
from taxonomic content and combine and take

00:12:56.949 --> 00:13:02.819
the intersection of them, thereby getting
a small data set of runs that they can run

00:13:02.819 --> 00:13:08.540
more expensive computational analysis on.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:11.009
Getting started is fairly easy.

00:13:11.009 --> 00:13:17.009
What you will get out of this will be customized
filtering for datasets that you want to emulate.

00:13:17.009 --> 00:13:22.790
Obviously you will have powerful computing
available to you.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:27.389
All you need is a laptop to login into the
cloud architecture.

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:33.939
If you are worried about costs, Amazon and
Google have free tiers available for you can

00:13:33.939 --> 00:13:40.309
start playing around with this technology
and moving your analysis pipeline or processes

00:13:40.309 --> 00:13:42.249
into the cloud.

00:13:42.249 --> 00:13:47.820
If you are working with small data sets on
small machines, all of this can be free for

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:48.980
you.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.529
definitely don't be worried about this.

00:13:52.529 --> 00:14:00.029
If you need to get data out of the cloud that
is called egress and you should only be egress

00:14:00.029 --> 00:14:03.699
and things that are the results of your analysis.

00:14:03.699 --> 00:14:11.679
That is much smaller in size than other raw
data or meta files you might generate between

00:14:11.679 --> 00:14:12.679
different tools.

00:14:12.679 --> 00:14:21.730
If you are ready to get started, we made videos
available on how to start an instance on Amazon

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:25.220
and Google so check out our YouTube channel
for that.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:26.220
https://tinyurl.com/SRAonthecloud

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:31.269
We have also provided help documentation for
all of this information in terms of getting

00:14:31.269 --> 00:14:38.259
started with computing and some example queries
for big query.

00:14:38.259 --> 00:14:41.010
You can find all of this in our help documentation.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:42.010
https://tinyurl.com/SRAcloudDoc

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:49.110
Now that I introduce the tools, we are always
looking for more feedback from the scientific

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:51.019
community.

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:55.940
Reach out to us and tell us if something works
or doesn't work for you.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:56.940
that's more important.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:03.170
If we know what is not working we can try
to fix it and make the tools better for you.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:10.790
the more feedback we get on anything either
for SRA or NCBI, we will take the feedback

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:14.550
and improve resources to meet your needs.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:21.160
If you want to continue following NCBI and
what we are releasing into the cloud you can

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:28.649
follow the NCBI insights blog where we will
post more information about upcoming new functionality.

00:15:28.649 --> 00:15:34.790
We also send out messages on Twitter and you
can follow NCBI for that.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:42.160
SRA is also registered with Amazon open data
registry so you can find us there as well.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:43.160
That is about it.

