00:19:39.389 --> 00:19:44.880
We have some example queries available on
documentation pages so you can copy those

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.789
to get started.

00:19:46.789 --> 00:19:53.049
There is a lot of useful tutorials online
for using sequel and learning sequel.

00:19:53.049 --> 00:19:57.130
To get started and it's not very hard.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:02.750
I have a more complex query here but as you
saw, I ran the query and the results came

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:05.570
back in seconds.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:09.940
You can see that I selected different type
of runs.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:16.530
Either from DDBJ for this one and this was
submitted to SRA.

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:22.880
I displayed some columns that may be useful
to me such as the number of spots used in

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:28.470
them and percent unidentified reads.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:33.710
Between the performance of big query and the
ability to write your own queries and customize

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:41.050
the results, this is a very useful tool for
pipeline developers or even if you just want

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:46.940
to get access to the data quickly, you can
use the web interface to search for your data

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:48.750
sets.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:53.120
I think it is a powerful tool that can be
very beneficial.

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:58.980
[Peter] Thank you Yuriy, we have a few more
