asked that question to talk to you more off-line

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:17.850
about that topic.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:20.880
[Peter] That's a good idea.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.870
You can write into the webinars address if
you want to.

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:25.370
Webinars at ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:32.070
And someone else asked can you show an example
of doing an assembly in the cloud.

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:34.899
For a particular SRR.

00:23:34.899 --> 00:23:42.200
[Yuriy] Doing assemblbly in the cloud is not
any different than running assembly at your

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:43.200
local server.

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:49.070
You use the same tools as you would there
as well.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:54.820
The only difference is that instead of running
it at local server that you have available

00:23:54.820 --> 00:24:00.490
to you or your desktop, you are doing it on
a server located in the cloud.

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:04.809
The process itself is not any different.

00:24:04.809 --> 00:24:10.580
In this case I would be calling up an assembler
and providing data to it.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:18.639
I don't have a demo available at this moment
but I'm sure that SRA can make a demo of that

00:24:18.639 --> 00:24:20.409
and share it.

00:24:20.409 --> 00:24:21.980
[Peter] Okay.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:24.629
Another question which I think you addressed
this.

00:24:24.629 --> 00:24:29.500
In some ways this webinar was about this topic,
but it is probably worth reiterating.

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:34.990
What is the advantage of using an ec2 instance
instead of using my own computer, basically

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:39.530
using the could instead of this person's own
computer.

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:43.210
Maybe there are a couple of points to you
share.

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:44.700
[Yuriy] Yes.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:51.909
If you are downloading data using the toolkit,
it usually pulls data from our servers but

00:24:51.909 --> 00:24:59.289
that is also where everybody around the world
is accessing the data you so are competing

00:24:59.289 --> 00:25:00.470
for resources.

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:04.890
In the cloud it scales basically automatically.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:09.590
You get faster access to the data there as
well.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:15.929
Also if you need more powerful computers not
available locally, you have those machines

00:25:15.929 --> 00:25:19.210
available in the cloud as well.

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:27.039
As I said, we had those bigger machines that
had 96 cores and lots of RAM.

00:25:27.039 --> 00:25:31.870
Those might be harder to get locally for you
but they are available to you easily on the

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:34.110
cloud architecture.

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:40.320
Those are the benefits of doing that where
you get faster access to data and more powerful

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.549
computes.

00:25:41.549 --> 00:25:49.420
If you move your pipelines and processes there
you can see it speed up in the overall analysis.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:51.100
[Peter] Thank you.

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:59.250
Good question and a good way to seal things
to give the main advantages of doing this.

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:01.529
We mention access to the conttigs now available.

00:26:01.529 --> 00:26:06.780
Is it easy for you to show us how to get access?

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:12.620
[Yuriy] Sure.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:23.269
Here I'm going to the virus discovery github
page.

00:26:23.269 --> 00:26:27.929
There is a readme on how to get access.

00:26:27.929 --> 00:26:33.779
We provide instructions of how you would want
to download the contigs.

00:26:33.779 --> 00:26:40.779
In this case if you are somewhere in the cloud,
either Google or Amazon, you can run either

00:26:40.779 --> 00:26:51.390
AWS s3 copy command and download the conttig
file to your processing area in Amazon, there

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:56.230
are similar commands with gsutils for Google.

00:26:56.230 --> 00:27:03.220
You can even run a wget command from a cloud
instance running at Amazon to download the

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:04.260
data.

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:14.730
That is all it takes, is running the one command
to download one of the contig fasta files.

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:16.669
[Peter] Okay.

00:27:16.669 --> 00:27:18.620
That's great.

